      'how-to-care-after': {
        title: 'How to Care for Patients After Artificial Knee Replacement Surgery?',
        shot: 'For those with severe knee osteoarthritis leading to bowed legs, excruciating pain that prevents walking, or whose symptoms have not improved after prolonged medication or injections, total knee replacement surgery is currently the best option.',
        detail: `<!-- wp:image {"align":"center","id":2689,"sizeSlug":"large","linkDestination":"none"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large"><img src="https://doctorkeng.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/ผู้ป่วยหลังการผ่าตัดเปลี่ยนข้อเข่าเทียม-จะดูแลกันอย่างไร_-1024x576.png" alt="ผู้ป่วยหลังการผ่าตัดเปลี่ยนข้อเข่าเทียม จะดูแลกันอย่างไร ?" class="wp-image-2689"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:heading {"textAlign":"center"} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-align-center">How to Care for Patients After Artificial Knee Replacement Surgery?</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"align":"center"} -->
<p class="has-text-align-center">By Asst. Prof. Dr. Thaninnit Leerapun</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>For those with severe knee osteoarthritis leading to bowed legs, excruciating pain that prevents walking, or whose symptoms have not improved after prolonged medication or injections, total knee replacement surgery is currently the best option.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":10192,"sizeSlug":"large","linkDestination":"none"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://doctorkeng.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/5ec4fbabb58b500503edaf3f-816x1024.jpeg" alt="" class="wp-image-10192"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":10193,"sizeSlug":"large","linkDestination":"none"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://doctorkeng.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/5ec4fb7663137b0c6af6e72f-775x1024.jpeg" alt="" class="wp-image-10193"/><figcaption class="wp-element-caption"></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->
<figcaption class="wp-element-caption">severely bowed legs</figcaption>
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>X-ray shows bone-on-bone, no cartilage.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":10194,"sizeSlug":"large","linkDestination":"none"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://doctorkeng.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/5ec4fc1efa1638056b81e383-962x1024.jpeg" alt="" class="wp-image-10194"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Why undergo surgery?</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Surgery is performed to reduce pain, correct the deformity of bowed legs, help straighten the legs, provide stability when walking to prevent easy falls, and enable you to perform daily activities better than before the surgery.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:embed {"url":"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLRmpWdWX6cKqJ-OlvYWelerQkoWnoLVad","type":"video","providerNameSlug":"youtube","responsive":true,"className":"wp-embed-aspect-16-9 wp-has-aspect-ratio"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-embed is-type-video is-provider-youtube wp-block-embed-youtube wp-embed-aspect-16-9 wp-has-aspect-ratio"><div class="wp-block-embed__wrapper">
https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLRmpWdWX6cKqJ-OlvYWelerQkoWnoLVad
</div></figure>
<!-- /wp:embed -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>After surgery, you can get up and walk the next day, using a cane for support.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":10197,"sizeSlug":"large","linkDestination":"none"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://doctorkeng.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/5ec4ffb366169f04e5205a5c-768x1024.jpeg" alt="" class="wp-image-10197"/><figcaption class="wp-element-caption"></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->
<figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Post-operative X-ray of artificial knee joint" or "X-ray after total knee replacement surgery.</figcaption>
<!-- wp:image {"id":10198,"sizeSlug":"large","linkDestination":"none"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://doctorkeng.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/5ec4fdde0872aa055020da5f-894x1024.jpeg" alt="" class="wp-image-10198"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>After surgery, local pain medication and nerve blocks will be administered to reduce pain.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>During the first day after surgery, we will apply cold packs to the operated knee to reduce swelling, inflammation, and pain. Patients can practice ankle pumps to move their leg muscles, which helps reduce the risk of blood clots.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>On Day 1 after surgery, you can get up, practice walking, go to the bathroom, and exercise your quadriceps muscles.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Post-operative physical therapy includes:</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Gait training using a walker for assistance</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Cold compression on the operated knee to reduce inflammation and pain</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Practicing full knee flexion and extension</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Quadriceps strengthening exercises</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":10199,"sizeSlug":"large","linkDestination":"none"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://doctorkeng.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/5ec4fe6d51398a0cb1b5c91d-1024x766.jpeg" alt="" class="wp-image-10199"/><figcaption class="wp-element-caption"></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->
<figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Quadriceps strengthening exercises (images for www.doctorkeng.com only)</figcaption>
<!-- wp:image {"id":10200,"sizeSlug":"large","linkDestination":"none"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://doctorkeng.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/5ec4fe87fa1638056b8421cd-1024x768.jpeg" alt="" class="wp-image-10200"/><figcaption class="wp-element-caption"></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->
<figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Quadriceps strengthening exercises using ankle weights (images for www.doctorkeng.com only)</figcaption>
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>It usually takes about 3 months to be able to walk normally without a cane. However, this depends on the patient's ability to perform physical therapy and muscle strengthening exercises.
</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Those who undergo surgery can drive after they are free of pain and can flex and extend their knee well.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>More than 80-95% of patients who undergo the surgery are satisfied with the results of their total knee replacement.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator {"className":"is-style-wide"} -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity is-style-wide"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"fontSize":"small"} -->
<p class="has-small-font-size">Reference:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"fontSize":"small"} -->
<p class="has-small-font-size">Patient Satisfaction After Total Knee Arthroplasty: Who Is Satisfied and Who Is Not?<br>Robert B Bourne 1 , Bert M Chesworth, Aileen M Davis, Nizar N Mahomed, Kory D J Charron</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->`,
      },
